"""
fxlms_anc_sim.py

Standalone Python reimplementation of the browser demo's FxLMS active-noise-
control core (sim.js), reworked for offline study instead of real-time
browser playback:

  * 48 kHz sample rate instead of the browser's 4 kHz. Every sample-domain
    constant from sim.js (filter lengths, path delays, decay time constants,
    smoothing-filter poles, warm-up/hold windows) is rescaled to preserve
    its original *physical* duration at the new rate -- see scale_len() /
    scale_pole() below. Frequencies expressed in Hz are untouched, since
    they're already sample-rate independent.
  * A realistic adaptive-filter length (default 384 taps @ 48 kHz = 8 ms,
    the same physical span as sim.js's 32 taps @ 4 kHz -- override with
    --taps for a longer/shorter control filter).
  * Swept across 6 independently-drawn (primary, secondary) acoustic path
    pairs instead of the browser's single fixed path, so results are
    reported as a mean +/- spread across paths rather than one draw.

Algorithm, unchanged from sim.js:
  - FxLMS feed-forward canceller using a fixed secondary-path *estimate*
    Shat (deliberately mismatched from the true secondary path S, like a
    real system would be).
  - An energy-ratio impulse detector with a warm-up period, so it doesn't
    trip on its own un-converged background estimate at startup.
  - Three adaptation variants:
      A: vanilla LMS, no robustness.
      B: robust (Geman-McClure-style) error weighting, always on.
      C: robust weighting + step-size cut, but ONLY while the detector is
         tripped or holding -- so C should track A during quiet periods
         and only pay the "robustness tax" during impulsive events.

Normalization modes (--norm):
  - 'leaky' (sim.js-faithful): the power normalizer pnorm is a leaky average
    with a ~250 ms time constant (rescaled from sim.js's 0.999 pole), capped
    to move at most 4x per sample. This is exactly what the browser demo
    does. It is deliberately conservative -- at a realistic 384-tap filter,
    stability requires mu as small as ~2e-5, which makes the whole loop
    adapt slowly (recovery-time metrics can fail to settle within a several-
    second trace).
  - 'instant' (textbook NLMS, default here): pnorm is just the current
    window's reference power, no leak. This is the standard NLMS
    normalization. Raw stability ceiling is mu~0.005 at 384 taps, but the
    hardest of the 6 default paths only has real margin below ~7e-4 (the
    default) -- still roughly 30x larger than the leaky mode's mu, so
    roughly 30x faster adaptation, at the cost of losing sim.js's extra
    smoothing safeguard against a single impulse briefly hijacking the
    normalizer.

Usage:
    python3 fxlms_anc_sim.py                      # default sweep, prints a table
    python3 fxlms_anc_sim.py --taps 512 --paths 6 --duration 5 --csv out.csv
    python3 fxlms_anc_sim.py --norm leaky --mu 2e-5   # reproduce sim.js's own normalizer
"""